  "notes": [
    "the text was unwrapped from an RTF document, as a text editor saves a terminal program's capture. Its groups, control words and escapes were taken out, and its paragraph and line breaks were read as the download's lines. A line named is the document's",
    "`T` read in milliseconds, from the step and the coast: the median step between rows is 250. The reader reads one above 5 as milliseconds. At the manuals' 1 to 33 samples a second, a step is 0.03 to 1 in seconds and 30 to 1,000 in milliseconds. On that clock `Alt` slows at 0.98 g in feet over the rows from 2.75 s to 5.75 s, up to the row before the marker `Apogee` at 6 s. The reader takes 0.7 to 1.3 g in feet or in meters, and a clock 1,000 times off would put it 10⁶ times off",
    "`Alt` read in feet, from the manual and the coast: the Classic and TRS manuals (p37) state feet. The Quantum and Proton manuals give every height in feet, and no Eggtimer manual offers meters. Over the rows from 2.75 s to 5.75 s, up to the row before the marker `Apogee` at 6 s, it slows at 0.98 g in feet, within the 0.7 to 1.3 g the reader takes. In meters it would be 3.214 g",
    "`Veloc` read in feet per second, from the manual: the Classic and TRS manuals (p37) give the velocity in ft/sec. The Proton's (p45) gives it as the change in `Alt` over the step. It is the change in `Alt` over the step, within 1 % or 1 ft/s. That holds in 43 of 43 rows after the first, where the reader asks 90 %",
    "`FAlt` read in feet, from the manual and the coast: it is the logger's filtered `Alt` (Quantum p38, Proton p45), in feet. Over the rows from 2.75 s to 5.75 s, up to the row before the marker `Apogee` at 6 s, it slows at 0.98 g in feet, within the 0.7 to 1.3 g the reader takes",
    "`FVeloc` read in feet per second, from the manual: the Proton's manual (p46) gives it as the filtered altitude's change over the step. The Excel guide (p7) graphs the velocity in ft/sec. It is the change in `FAlt` over the step, within 1 % or 1 ft/s. That holds in 43 of 43 rows after the first, where the reader asks 90 %",
    "the marker columns, kept as written: `LDA`, `LowV`, `Apogee`, `N-O`, `Drogue` and `Main`. Each is 0 but at the sample that marks its event. There the Quantum's and Proton's manuals (p38, p46) put the raw altitude, in feet",
    "`ASL Alt.` read in feet, from its unit mark: the flight summary writes 800 ft; the launch site's elevation above sea level, as the logger measured it (TRS manual p36)",
    "`Apogee` read in feet, from its unit mark: the flight summary writes 483 ft; the logger's apogee above its zero, kept beside the table's altitudes (TRS manual p36)",
    "`T @` and `Time @` read in seconds, from the manual: the TRS manual's download writes the flight summary's times in `secs.` (p36). The same download writes its LDA at 0.700 secs. as \"700ms into the flight\" (p37)",
    "the flight summary's times are its table markers' to the millisecond, one event each, the marker's: `Apogee` at `Apogee`, `LDA` at `LDA`, `N-O` at `N-O`",
    "the flight summary's times past the table's last, 9 s, kept as written and not read as events. No event of the flight comes after its last sample, so they aren't in seconds: `Unlock` 9500",
    "the flight settings, as the file writes them: `Drogue <1=Normal,2=Backup,0=OFF>` 1; `Main Altitude <50-2000 by 50, 0=OFF>` 300; `Bench mode {test}` 2",
    "1 line of the flight settings holds no setting: the name its owner gave the flight computer (TRS manual p24). The name is left out of the record as free text that may name a person",
    "the flight summary's other lines, as the file writes them: `Base Temp: 61.0 °F`; `Last Flight Phase: 9` (landing detected, TRS manual p37); `Max Velocity: 161 ft/sec`",
    "1 lines before the title `Eggtimer … Flight Settings` weren't read",
    "the document holds 2 Eggtimer downloads one after another. The first was read, as a record holds one flight. The titles of those that weren't are on lines 77"
